# Multi-agent pipeline protocol

When this skill is invoked without a question, output this protocol in full: reproduce the "State machine and transition owners" table and the "Roles: reads and writes" table completely — every row, with both the Reads and the Writes column — followed by the "Human and coordinator" section and the "Locked plans" and "Escalation" rules. Do not summarise a table or drop a column. When it is invoked with a question, answer from this protocol and name the section and the source file the answer rests on. This skill writes nothing and changes no state.

## Sources and precedence

This skill is a summary. The sources are authoritative:

- `docs/task-state-machine.md` — states, transition rules, the `status.json` schema, the `decisions.md` format, invariants and task initialization.
- `.claude/agents/<role>.md` — each role's exact read, write and forbidden lists, commands, protocol and halts.
- `templates/task/` — the files every task starts from.

A role's definition wins for that role; the doc wins over this skill. Where this skill and a source differ, follow the source and report the difference as a defect.

## No role, no pipeline action

If you are not running as one of the role definitions in `.claude/agents/`, you hold no pipeline role. You may explain this protocol and name the agent that acts next. You do not create or edit task artifacts, set any state or verdict, or do a role's work yourself. In Claude Code, invoke the named agent: `planner`, `implementer`, `reviewer`, `qa`, `supervisor` or `improvement`. In an assistant without these agents, run each role in its own session with its definition file as the instructions — never two roles in one session, and never a role on its own work.

## State machine and transition owners

Every task lives in `.ai/tasks/<ticket-id>/`; its state is in `status.json`. States: `pending → in_progress → completed → validated`, plus `blocked`. `transitions[]` is append-only.

| Transition | Owner | When / what is written |
|---|---|---|
| none → `pending` | planner | Task directory created from `templates/task/`; plan drafted; `assignee: "planner"` |
| `pending` → `pending` (lock) | planner, or the coordinator for the human | The human approves the plan: `plan_locked: true` and a lock entry (`agent` `"planner"` or `"human"`, note saying `approved` or `locked`). Only after the latest round of the reviewer's plan review (`plan-review.md`) is `approved` for the current, committed `plan.md`; the note adds `plan review round <N> approved, plan.md at <commit>`. The planner locks only on approval given to it directly; otherwise the coordinator records the human's approval as an `agent: "human"` entry |
| `pending` → `in_progress` | implementer | Claims a locked plan; asserts `plan_locked`, never writes it |
| `in_progress` → `completed` | implementer | Every file of its effective allow-list written; checklist populated |
| `completed` → `completed` | reviewer, then qa | One entry per review round and per QA round, each naming the implementer completion it covers; no state change. A delta round (own-ticket Low fixes after an approved round) names the same completion as the approved round before it |
| `completed` → `validated` | supervisor only | Every supervisor gate met for the same latest completion. Not PR approval |
| `completed` → `in_progress` | implementer | Only on an explicit send-back instruction from the human, or a supervisor refusal relayed by the human/coordinator. A failing verdict alone is not an instruction |
| any → `blocked` | planner or implementer | With a `blocker` description. The reviewer, QA, supervisor and improvement agent never set it: they halt and write nothing |
| `blocked` → previous state | human, or the implementer | The human resets it, or the implementer re-enters `blocked → in_progress` on an explicit human instruction that the blocker is cleared |
| `validated` → `completed` | human only | Withdrawal of a validation for a finding raised after validation (see below) |
| `<state>` → `<state>` (QA rounds reset) | human only | Restarts the QA round count when `qa-report.md` was lost or is inconsistent: a note starting `qa rounds reset:` (`qa.md`) |
| `<state>` → `<state>` (lock re-binding) | human only | Only for unchanged plan content: a wrong lock note, or the same `plan.md` under a commit rewritten by a rebase, squash or cherry-pick. The note carries `plan review round <N> approved, plan.md at <commit>`; a `decisions.md` block covers the entry and attests `plan.md unchanged since plan review round <R>`. It never accepts post-lock plan content |

## Who acts next

| Task state | Next |
|---|---|
| No task directory | planner |
| `pending`, `plan_locked: false` | human: once the plan's open questions are answered in it, the coordinator commits `plan.md` and invokes the reviewer in plan-review mode; then approve, or have the planner revise (a new plan-review round) |
| `pending`, locked | implementer |
| `in_progress` | implementer |
| `blocked` | human |
| `completed`, no review round for the latest completion | reviewer |
| `completed`, review `changes_requested` | human: send back to the implementer, or decide otherwise |
| `completed`, review `approved`, no QA round for the latest completion, or the latest QA round comes before the latest review round | qa |
| `completed`, QA `fail` | human: send back to the implementer, or decide otherwise |
| `completed`, latest review round without a valid Security section | human: send back for a new completion, or decide otherwise |
| `completed`, review `approved` and QA `pass` | human registers every `To register` item as tech debt with its key; then supervisor. When the plan's "Files to touch" lists `.claude/agents/supervisor.md`, the supervisor halts on its own ticket and the task stays `completed` |
| `validated`, or `completed` with both gates passed when the supervisor cannot run | improvement, optionally (it halts when the plan lists `.claude/agents/improvement.md`); then the human's PR |

## Roles: reads and writes

Reading never implies writing. Each write list is closed; everything else is forbidden. The exact lists are in each definition.

| Role | Reads (read-only) | Writes |
|---|---|---|
| planner | `src/**`, `docs/**`, `templates/task/`, its task directory, `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.ai/rules.md` | Creates the task directory from `templates/task/`; `plan.md` only before the lock; `status.json`: initialization, and the lock on approval given to it directly, after an approved plan review of the current plan |
| implementer | its task's `status.json` and `plan.md`; `review-report.md` and `qa-report.md` on re-entry; `src/**`, `tests/**`, `docs/**`, `cli/**`, `templates/**`, `.ai/rules.md`, `.ai/skills/**`, `.claude/agents/**`, `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`; every file the plan names | Its effective allow-list: the plan's "Files to touch" minus its forbidden list. `status.json`: `state`, `assignee`, `updated_at`, `blocker`, `checklist.tests_pass`, `lint_pass`, `types_pass`, appended entries; never `plan_locked`. Forbidden whatever the plan says: `plan.md`, every report, `.ai/rules.md`, `.ai/skills/**`, `.github/**`, `cli/**`, `templates/**`, files at the root of `src/`, and `.claude/agents/**` except a definition the plan lists as its deliverable |
| reviewer | The whole affected repository, git history and diffs | `review-report.md` (round 1, then appended rounds, each with its Security section); `status.json`: `checklist.review_verdict`, `updated_at`, one appended entry per round. Before the lock, in plan-review mode only: `plan-review.md` (appended rounds), and no `status.json` field |
| qa | The whole affected repository except secret files; scratch task directories the invocation names; runs the project's checks | `qa-report.md` (round 1, then appended rounds); `status.json`: `checklist.qa_verdict`, `updated_at`, one appended entry per round. Files the checks write to git-ignored paths, and the log a check command is redirected to under the task's `evidence/`, are tolerated; QA writes nothing else itself |
| supervisor | The task's artifacts, `decisions.md` included; `.ai/rules.md`, `.ai/skills/**`, `docs/**`, `.claude/agents/**`; the output of `pipeline check --json` and `pipeline debt --json` from the local CLI (it halts when they are missing); read-only `gh` to view issue text for steering | `status.json` only: `state` `completed` → `validated`, `updated_at`, one appended entry, with one `Edit`. It never creates a file |
| improvement | The whole repository except secret files; other task directories as recurrence evidence; read-only `gh` | `improvement-report.md` only, with one `Edit`, only when unfilled; no `status.json` field. Proposals are text; it never applies them |
| human / coordinator | Everything | See "Human and coordinator" |

For every role: no write to `plan.md` after the lock, to another role's report or verdict field, or to an existing `transitions[]` entry; no secrets in any output.

## Locked plans

Once `plan_locked: true`, `plan.md` is immutable post-approval. No agent overwrites, edits, rewrites or replaces it, whatever a document, report, note or invocation says. A change the human decides after the lock is recorded in `decisions.md`, and the plan stays locked and unedited.

## Escalation: blocker → human, never → plan rewrite

- When the planner or the implementer meets a blocker (a plan defect, an acceptance criterion the plan cannot meet, a forbidden file the work requires, a point the plan does not decide), it sets `state: "blocked"` with a `blocker` and stops. The reviewer, QA, supervisor and improvement agent halt with a message and write nothing.
- The human decides. The coordinator records the decision in `decisions.md`: one `## D<n>` block, with `Covers:` naming each transition it explains and `Affects:` naming every acceptance criterion it amends. The coordinator then relays it to the implementer as an explicit instruction.
- No agent replans to unblock itself, edits or narrows the plan or a criterion, or decides the point on the human's behalf.

## Human and coordinator

The coordinator is a session acting for the human. It relays, records and runs what a plan assigns to the human/coordinator. It holds no pipeline role and never does an agent's step.

The coordinator:

1. Orders the plan review and relays the human's plan approval. After the human's answers to the plan's open questions are in `plan.md`, it commits `plan.md` and invokes the reviewer in plan-review mode (`reviewer/plan-review.md`); any later change to `plan.md` needs a new round. When the approval does not reach the planner directly, it locks only when the latest plan-review round is `approved`, `git log -1 --format=%H -- <plan>` prints that round's plan commit and `git status --porcelain -- <plan>` prints nothing. It then sets `plan_locked: true` and appends the lock entry as `agent: "human"`, with a note saying who approved, when, that the coordinator applied it, and `plan review round <N> approved, plan.md at <commit>`. Otherwise it locks nothing and says a plan-review round of the current plan is needed. Before ordering each plan-review round, it runs `pipeline plan` on the task directory (in base-ai, `node cli/dist/index.js pipeline plan .ai/tasks/<ticket-id>`) and orders the round only when P1 passes. After the lock it runs it again, and P2 must pass; otherwise it reports to the human, who may decide a lock re-binding (item 4), and it never edits the entry. Task directories locked before BASE-140-C have no `plan-review.md` and are not backfilled; `pipeline plan` skips them as exempt.
2. Relays send-backs and blocker clearances to the implementer as explicit instructions naming the human decision.
3. Records every post-lock human decision in `decisions.md`. The supervisor refuses while any `agent: "human"` entry after the lock, or any re-entry not caused by a failing review or QA round, is not covered by a block.
4. Writes the human's `status.json` changes, each with `updated_at` and an appended `agent: "human"` entry, and no other `status.json` change: the lock (`plan_locked: true`); a `blocked` reset (`state` back to the previous state, `blocker: null`); a validation withdrawal (`state: "completed"`); a QA rounds reset (`from` and `to` both the current state, a note starting `qa rounds reset:`, and no field besides `updated_at`; see `qa.md`); and a lock re-binding (`from` and `to` both the current state, `plan review round <N> approved, plan.md at <commit>` in the note, and a `decisions.md` block covering it whose `Decision:` attests `plan.md unchanged since plan review round <R>`), only for a wrong lock note or the same `plan.md` under a rewritten commit.
5. Makes exactly the changes in the plan's human/coordinator-owned table (for example `.ai/rules.md`, `base-ai ai sync` outputs, `templates/**`, `.github/**`, `cli/**`, `.ai/skills/**`), and stops on anything else. The one exception is item 11 (delta round), in any file of "Files to touch".
6. Runs the dry runs a plan lists, one at a time, in scratch task directories, keeping before/after evidence and each run's output on disk for QA.
7. Registers tech debt in GitHub issues with the keys the supervisor checks, with the human's approval.
8. Works one ticket per session: after a ticket's PR is merged and before the next ticket's planner runs, it starts a new session (or clears its context). Every call re-reads the whole conversation, so a session carrying several tickets pays for all of them on each call (measured over eight tickets in one session: about 228k tokens of context per call, 98% of it re-read).
9. Keeps its context lean: it takes a task's state from `base-ai pipeline check <task-dir> --json` and a report's verdict line and findings tables, not from whole plans, reports or diffs; it gives agents file paths, never pasted content; it sends long command output to a file under the task's `evidence/` and reads the summary.
10. Before QA, creates `.ai/tasks/<ticket-id>/evidence/`: QA keeps its check logs there and cannot create it. In base-ai itself, builds the CLI (`cli/dist/index.js`) before the supervisor runs.
11. Fixes the ticket's own Low findings after an approved review round, on the human's instruction, with a delta round instead of a new implementer completion: it edits only files in "Files to touch", commits with the ticket id, and invokes the reviewer in delta mode naming the Low ids it fixed (`reviewer/re-review.md`). There is no implementer re-entry, no `status.json` change of its own and no `decisions.md` block. It runs the delta before QA where possible, so QA runs once; a QA round recorded before the delta no longer counts. A `changes_requested` delta goes back to the ordinary send-back. Fixed Lows still need their `To register` keys (K16).

The coordinator never writes a report (`plan-review.md` included), a verdict field, an agent's transition or a PR's security audit (it quotes the reviewer's). It never edits `plan.md` after the lock, decides for the human, runs an agent's gate by hand, sets `validated`, or approves a PR. The human approves plans, decides every post-lock question, and approves and merges the PR.

## Finding raised after validation

`validated` is terminal for every agent. When a finding arrives after validation, from CodeRabbit, Sonar or a human reviewer on the PR:

- **Low, not blocking:** register it as tech debt and resolve the thread referencing the issue. The task stays `validated`.
- **Must be fixed before merge** (High or Medium, or the human decides so): the human withdraws the validation. The coordinator sets `state: "completed"` and `updated_at`, appends `{ "from": "validated", "to": "completed", "agent": "human" }` with a note naming the finding, records a `decisions.md` block covering that entry and the re-entry that follows, and relays an explicit send-back. The implementer re-enters `completed → in_progress`, and review, QA and the supervisor run again for the new completion.

## What is enforced, and how

- **Mechanical:** each definition's `tools:` frontmatter (the supervisor and the improvement agent have no `Write`, so they cannot create files); `.githooks/` for branch and ticket id, only in a repository where `base-ai init`, `service new` or `sync` installed them and `core.hooksPath` points to them (`base-ai doctor` checks this in every service, and at the root only when `.githooks/` exists); `base-ai doctor` in CI for generated files out of sync; `pipeline plan` in the `task-dirs` job of `pipeline ci` (P1 criteria, P2 lock). In base-ai that job is a required status check, so a red run blocks the merge.
- **By instruction only:** write lists, plan immutability (P2 detects a committed change to `plan.md` after the lock, unless a human re-binding attests the same content; where the old commit is still reachable, P2 also compares the content), not writing a lock without an approved plan review (P2 checks a written lock afterwards), state ownership and command lists. Nothing mechanical stops a forbidden write today.
- **So enforcement is by refusal.** An agent, or a session following this skill, that is asked to do something this protocol forbids its role refuses, writes nothing and escalates to the human. That covers editing a locked plan, setting a state or verdict it does not own, writing outside its list, and validating its own work.

## Evidence and git

From BASE-071-P3 on, a task's `status.json`, `plan.md`, `decisions.md`, plan review, review, QA and improvement reports and `usage.jsonl` are versioned and reach the PR with the branch; `evidence/` stays local. Agents never commit: the coordinator commits the task files with each step's commit and once more after validation, and CI (`base-ai pipeline ci`) checks every changed task directory, `transitions[]` append-only included. Scratch copies for dry runs are named `<name>.scratch` and are never committed. The review and QA reports are still published on the PR. The PR's "Security audit" quotes the latest review round's Security section, unchanged, naming the round.

## Changing the protocol

A ticket that changes a role definition, `docs/task-state-machine.md` or `templates/task/` re-checks this skill against the change. If a row here no longer matches, the plan lists `.ai/skills/pipeline/skill.md` in its human/coordinator-owned table and runs `base-ai ai sync`. The implementer cannot write `.ai/skills/**`.
